## How to Tell Your Nail Tech Exactly What You Want

A folder of screenshots is good. Knowing what you're actually asking for is better.

The confusion at most salon appointments comes from the vocabulary overlap between jelly, milky, glazed, and sheer. These terms describe related but distinct finishes, and most clients (understandably) use them interchangeably. The shorthand that gets you what you want, every time: *"I want a jelly French. The base should be translucent, like you can see through it, not milky or creamy. Very glossy. Then a [colour] French tip."*

That distinction (translucent, not milky) is the one that matters. Milky is opaque-adjacent. Jelly is see-through. Tell your tech which, and show a reference image alongside it.

For the tip itself, the most useful descriptors: micro-tip (barely-there line at the free edge), standard tip (classic French width), or blurred tip (a slightly diffused edge, no hard line). Specifying tip width prevents the outcome that frustrates most people: a French manicure that overwhelms a shorter nail or reads as too 1990s on a modern length.

This is also the point to mention nail shape. A well-applied <strong>jelly french manicure</strong> on the wrong shape for your hand can look completely different from the inspo image you brought. Your nail tech will guide you, but knowing the names (squoval, almond, oval, coffin) means the conversation starts from a clearer place. For product and formula direction, <a href='/blog/jelly-nails/jelly-nails'>Best Jelly Nail Polish to Buy Right Now</a> covers the specific sheer formulas worth asking for by name.

